            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Artistic Influences</strong><br>\nIngmar Bergman was born on July 14, 1918, in Uppsala, Sweden. His strict Lutheran upbringing and the rich storytelling of his nursemaid informed his later thematic concerns and narrative style. He attended Stockholm University and became involved in theater and writing, which paved the way for his film career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Entry into Filmmaking</strong><br>\nBergman began working in the Swedish film industry as a scriptwriter and assistant director in the early 1940s. He directed his first film, \"Crisis,\" in 1946, followed by \"It Rains on Our Love\" in 1946 and \"A Ship to India\" in 1947, honing his craft with each project.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Exploring Human Psyche and Existence</strong><br>\nBergman's films are renowned for their deep exploration of the human psyche, existential angst, and complex emotional landscapes. His 1957 film \"The Seventh Seal\" featured iconic imagery and tackled profound questions of faith and mortality, reflecting Bergman's own concerns.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
