            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Theatrical Influence and Narrative Technique</strong><br>\nA prominent theater director, Bergman's cinematic work was heavily influenced by his stage experience, with a focus on character introspection and minimalistic settings. His narrative techniques often involved direct address, tight close-ups, and long takes to engage the audience intimately with the characters' inner turmoil.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Frequent Collaborations with Actors</strong><br>\nBergman frequently collaborated with a repertory of actors, including Liv Ullmann, Bibi Andersson, and Max von Sydow. This close working relationship resulted in powerful performances and a consistent thread of authenticity and intensity across his films.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mid-Career Masterpieces</strong><br>\nIn the 1960s and 1970s, Bergman released some of his most acclaimed works, including \"Persona,\" \"Cries and Whispers,\" and \"Scenes from a Marriage.\" These films dealt with themes of identity, communication, and the complexity of relationships.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
