          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Film Autobiography</strong><br>\nBergman's personal life, including his marriages and relationships, often influenced his storytelling. His autobiographical film \"Fanny and Alexander\" in 1982 was both a reflection on his childhood and an intricate tapestry of the familial and spiritual themes that permeated his work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nBergman's influence extends beyond film to the broader cultural perception of Sweden and existential philosophy in art. His works continue to be a subject of study and reverence in the film community for their artistic depth and narrative innovation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Honors and Final Years</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Bergman received numerous awards and honors, including multiple Academy Awards and the Irving G. Thalberg Memorial Award. Until his death on July 30, 2007, Bergman remained a towering figure in the world of film, leaving behind a rich legacy as one of cinema's greatest auteurs.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
